uts."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"af4512c4bc8e1d0ae1300735"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which point lies on the graph of $f(x) = x^2$ with domain $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["$(2,3)$","$(4,2)$","$(2,4)$","$(1,2)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"797660ef234b3cb6fbd8bd5b"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which point lies on the graph of $f(x)=x^2$?","options":["$(2,2)$","$(4,2)$","$(-2,-4)$","$(2,4)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"09e23337c501269df11f202a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement follows from the fact that $f$ is a function?","options":["Each input corresponds to exactly one point on the graph.","Each input corresponds to at least two points on the graph.","The graph cannot be a subset of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","The graph contains no ordered pairs."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"55f1bcc1579326bb42a55c60"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The graph of $f\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$ is described as a subset of which product set?","options":["$\\mathbb{R}\\times X\\times X$","$X\\times\\mathbb{R}$","$X\\cup\\mathbb{R}$","$\\mathbb{R}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7e74adedc9fd2c806b3200cd"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $X = \\{0,1,2\\}$ and $f(x) = x+1$, which set is the graph of $f$?","options":["$\\{(1,0),(2,1),(3,2)\\}$","$\\{(0,1),(1,2),(2,3)\\}$","$\\{0,1,2,3\\}$","$\\{(0,0),(1,1),(2,2)\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fd79c39d692382410664b704"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = \\mathrm{e}^x$, which description matches its graph?","options":["Its graph grows rapidly.","Its graph is a horizontal line.","Its graph is empty.","Its graph has two outputs for every input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0eb2fe887c4207316ef13483"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can the same formula give different graphs when the domain changes?","options":["Because the graph ignores the domain.","Because the graph is always all of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","The graph includes only pairs $(x,f(x))$ with $x\\in X$.","Because each input may have many outputs."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0fa9afbec24f0d7dc61d9745"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which set cannot be the graph of a function from $X = \\{1\\}$ to $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["$\\{(1,2)\\}$","$\\{(1,2),(1,3)\\}$","$\\{(1,-1)\\}$","$\\{(1,0)\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f1d88b6593b6466e23645d19"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X = \\{1,2\\}$ and $f(1) = 3$, $f(2) = 3$. Which point is not on the graph?","options":["$(1,3)$","$(2,3)$","$(3,1)$","Both $(1,3)$ and $(2,3)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d49dd48edcf4276d1ade53e"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_graph_of_a_function_5a405391_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X = \\{-1,0,1\\}$ and $f(x) = x^2$. Which graph is correct?","options":["$\\{(-1,1),(0,0),(1,1)\\}$","$\\{(-1,-1),(0,0),(1,1)\\}$","$\\{(1,-1),(0,0),(1,1)\\}$","$\\{(-1,1),(0,1),(1,1)\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4640ede789552fd86c62d3c"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of objects make up the graph of a function?","options":["Only output values $f(x)$.","Only input values $x$.","Unordered triples.","Ordered pairs $(x,f(x))$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1429d52925cc78b098356544"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a point $(x,f(x))$ on the graph, what is the first coordinate?","options":["The input $x$.","The output $f(x)$.","The codomain only.","The derivative."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"23956b42723da712da51b327"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does each input correspond to exactly one point on the graph?","options":["Because every graph is a straight line.","A function assigns exactly one output per input.","Because inputs are not allowed in graphs.","Because every output has exactly one input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5aa876dfe0c05d41cca05cec"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which point lies on the graph of $f(x)=2x$?","options":["$(3,5)$","$(6,3)$","$(3,6)$","$(2,3)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6fb51e4abc846b10331b9e18"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $X=\\{-1,0,1\\}$ and $f(x)=x^2$, which set is the graph?","options":["$\\{(-1,-1),(0,0),(1,1)\\}$","$\\{-1,0,1\\}$","$\\{(1,-1),(0,0),(1,1)\\}$","$\\{(-1,1),(0,0),(1,1)\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c384039a761a771565a2e96a"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which point lies on the graph of $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^x$?","options":["$(0,0)$","$(1,0)$","$(-1,0)$","$(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"57429e0ea99958ce5bbea149"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can changing the domain change the graph even if the formula stays the same?","options":["The graph ignores the domain.","The graph uses only inputs from the domain.","The graph always equals all of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","The formula stops assigning outputs."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4ed158bdc6ee4ecb4285f488"},{"id":"graph_of_a_function_expansion_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If the graph of $f$ contains the point $(3,9)$, what must be true?","options":["$f(9)=3$","$f(3)=3$","$f(9)=9$","$f(3)=9$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a060a3552fdcfb08964c637"}]}}